em  so they use a lot less energy than  conventional designs do the system is  very energy efficient it's also  important for all the facade elements to  be well insulated the windows are  specially treated so they don't allow a  lot of heat to get into the building  that's very important here because we  have a lot of sunshine  it's a very demanding project and not  just because the first time we designed  I have a lot of international experience  so I know how architectural designs vary  from place to place but this is my first  opportunity to complete a project for a  big client in my home country it's  simply fantastic  more than five million people live in  Singapore that's about four times the  population of Panama City's metro area  most of Singapore's residents live in  high-rise buildings how can the people  who design these buildings help to  maintain or even improve the quality of  life in large Asian cities the whoo-hah  architectural firm was founded by one  one son and in Australian colleague  Richard hassel they specialize in  creating alternative solutions that  bring their high-rise buildings into  greater harmony with the surrounding  urban environment and not soulless steel  and glass structures that gobble energy  like there is no tomorrow  the most distinctive feature of  buildings designed by whoo-hah is the  greening of the external facade openings  and meeting rooms a concert hall  theaters and classrooms all under one  roof it's called sandwich and it's  energy efficient as Cheddington ozb  Ranaut explains the main parts of the  building were designed to maximize  natural ventilation so that even if  there's no wind's blowing outside  there'll always be a breeze inside we  also use natural ventilation in the  classrooms ceiling fans instead of air  conditioners there are also breeze ways  between the individual structures and  that also contributes to the natural  ventilation  most of the energy that's consumed in  the world's tropical regions is used to  power cooling systems but simple changes  to the architecture can create natural  ventilation systems that work so well  that air conditioning isn't needed a  good example of this can be found at  another high-rise structure designed by  whoo-ha  more main rise it counted a bay window  the monsoon window alright so this top  layer is just to prevent small things  from dropping true and then the second  layer is the slider and this is a slider  that allows you to open and close the  window and then you have the safety bars  and that's to prevent the size of 100  millimeter diameter ball from falling  true so that's a safety barrier so the  rain would come in but only yes only the  breeze would come in so the system  monsoon window that allows natural  ventilation 24 hours a day 365 days a  year and you'd really don't have to  worry about running to close a window  and it rings so we can see at a higher  level you you get the breeze and the  breeze is actually quite nice and strong  and slightly drier and lower perhaps  there are a few things that we can now  with this implementer we know what's the  problems with it and we can actually  improve them one of the things is  actually acoustic by opening the windows  in this way too we also allow some of  the noise to come up and this is  something that we think that we would  like to address as well this is Wong's  grand design for the city of the future  an integrated system of high-rises that  offers a sustainable environment for  homes and businesses  if you try to create a high density  solution you do have to go high-rise and  we really think what is really necessary  is to duplicate the city not on one  ground level but to replicate this on to  higher levels and into multiple levels  and you can see from from this rendering  to is that we have almost 200 to 250  stories and but what is really quite  important is within this lattice City at  this kind of height to get the quality  of good fresh air and natural light  sunlight is actually very important for  the city high-rise buildings are  undergoing a major transition in design  as architects develop new ways to  increase sustainability and energy  efficiency the world's major urban areas  will continue to reach for the sky but  the structures that dominate the skyline  will take on an increasingly green  character ecological high-rise  architecture is the buzzword and a key  technology for the future
 

